rules_python 2.4.2: make hermetic interpreter repos cacheable in the remote repo contents cache

python_repository creates a __pycache__ entry in every directory with .py files: a symlink to an
absolute path outside the repo ($XDG_CACHE_HOME/rules_python_pycache/..., or under TMP), or, when
no cache dir is available, a symlink to /dev/null. Bazel 9 never uploads a repo with symlinks
that leave the repo to the remote repo contents cache (RepositoryUtils.replantSymlinks ->
safeForRemoteCache=false, bazelbuild/bazel#30160), and skips the upload without a message. So
every interpreter repo misses the remote cache on every machine and every fresh output base.

This patch adds RULES_PYTHON_PYCACHE_DIR=/dev/null (or NUL) as a way to turn the pycache off, and
then writes __pycache__ as an empty regular file in place of the /dev/null symlink. The repo then
contains no symlinks, and it is uploaded and served lazily. Behavior without the env var is
unchanged. This matches upstream bazel-contrib/rules_python d03bc77c (#4264, on main, not in any
release as of 2.4.2).

Use: single_version_override(module_name = "rules_python", patch_strip = 1,
         patches = ["//patches:rules_python-2.4.2-pycache-regular-file.patch"])
     common --repo_env=RULES_PYTHON_PYCACHE_DIR=/dev/null
Verified with python_3_12_riscv64-unknown-linux-gnu, Bazel 9.2, a local REAPI cache and two fresh
output bases per variant:
  patched:   run 1 uploads (2 AC updates), run 2 is a lazy hit, 0 symlinks leave the repo
  unpatched: both runs miss and nothing is uploaded

diff --git a/python/private/python_repository.bzl b/python/private/python_repository.bzl
index 3fd0928a..86d0569a 100644
--- a/python/private/python_repository.bzl
+++ b/python/private/python_repository.bzl
@@ -65,6 +65,8 @@ def _get_pycache_root(rctx):
 
     # 1. RULES_PYTHON_PYCACHE_DIR
     res = rctx.getenv("RULES_PYTHON_PYCACHE_DIR")
+    if res and res.lower() in ("/dev/null", "nul"):
+        return None
     if res:
         res = res + "/" + rctx.name
         return repo_utils.mkdir(rctx, res)
@@ -107,9 +109,6 @@ def _create_pycache_symlinks(rctx, logger):
     logger.info(lambda: "pycache root: {}".format(pycache_root))
     pycache_root_str = str(pycache_root) if pycache_root else None
 
-    os_name = repo_utils.get_platforms_os_name(rctx)
-    null_device = "NUL" if os_name == "windows" else "/dev/null"
-
     queue = [rctx.path(".")]
 
     # Starlark doesn't support recursion, use a loop with a queue.
@@ -143,7 +142,13 @@ def _create_pycache_symlinks(rctx, logger):
                 rctx.symlink(target_dir, pycache_dir)
             else:
                 rctx.delete(pycache_dir)
-                rctx.symlink(null_device, pycache_dir)
+
+                # The intent is to disable pyc generation, which we can't
+                # entirely do directly. A symlink to the null device points
+                # outside the repo, which keeps the repo out of the remote repo
+                # contents cache. A regular file seems to make python skip
+                # local pyc generation, so use that.
+                rctx.file(pycache_dir, content = "", executable = False)
 
